1
00:00:00,148 --> 00:00:03,376
Hello, and a very warm welcome to Shah Spectrum.

2
00:00:03,376 --> 00:00:05,335
If you're new here, welcome.

3
00:00:05,335 --> 00:00:10,902
If you're back again, it's lovely to have you with us once more.

4
00:00:11,550 --> 00:00:12,933
Here is a puzzle:

5
00:00:12,933 --> 00:00:16,226
India's central bank is making cash scarce on purpose.

6
00:00:16,226 --> 00:00:19,884
In early September, banks had about eleven lakh crore rupees of spare cash.

7
00:00:19,884 --> 00:00:23,001
Now they have about forty-six thousand crore.

8
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:25,159
Why would the Reserve Bank want that?

9
00:00:25,992 --> 00:00:30,921
Hi, I'm Mittal Shah, and this is my A-I voice, telling you today's story.

10
00:00:31,652 --> 00:00:33,030
First, the numbers.

11
00:00:33,030 --> 00:00:36,567
The spare cash in the banking system is called liquidity.

12
00:00:36,567 --> 00:00:43,053
By one widely used estimate, it fell from about eleven lakh crore rupees in early

13
00:00:43,053 --> 00:00:47,884
September to about forty-six thousand crore by the twenty-sixth of October.

14
00:00:47,884 --> 00:00:51,836
That is a drop of roughly ninety-six per cent in under two months.

15
00:00:51,836 --> 00:00:59,160
And the price of borrowing for one night, the overnight rate, has moved close to the

16
00:00:59,160 --> 00:01:00,929
top of the Reserve Bank's range.

17
00:01:01,445 --> 00:01:03,410
So how did the Reserve Bank do it?

18
00:01:03,410 --> 00:01:04,010
In three steps.

19
00:01:04,311 --> 00:01:05,320
Step one, the rate.

20
00:01:05,320 --> 00:01:11,233
On the seventh of October it raised its main rate, the repo rate, by a quarter of a

21
00:01:11,233 --> 00:01:15,824
percentage point, to five and a half per cent.

22
00:01:15,824 --> 00:01:19,587
The repo rate is what the Reserve Bank charges when it lends to banks.

23
00:01:19,587 --> 00:01:21,766
It was the first rise in nearly four years.

24
00:01:22,252 --> 00:01:22,852
Step two, bonds.

25
00:01:22,821 --> 00:01:25,397
It sold government bonds into the market.

26
00:01:25,397 --> 00:01:29,407
When a buyer pays for a bond, cash leaves the banking system.

27
00:01:29,407 --> 00:01:30,506
So selling bonds drains cash.

28
00:01:30,987 --> 00:01:31,958
Step three, a rule.

29
00:01:31,958 --> 00:01:36,762
Banks must keep a share of their deposits with the Reserve Bank.

30
00:01:36,762 --> 00:01:38,206
This is called the cash reserve ratio.

31
00:01:38,206 --> 00:01:43,512
Until recently, on any single day a bank could drop to ninety per cent of the

32
00:01:43,512 --> 00:01:47,615
required amount, as long as the fortnight's average was met.

33
00:01:47,615 --> 00:01:52,429
From the sixteenth of October, the daily floor is ninety-nine per cent.

34
00:01:52,429 --> 00:01:55,038
That leaves banks almost no room to run short.

35
00:01:55,661 --> 00:01:57,796
There is one more piece.

36
00:01:57,796 --> 00:02:03,847
The Reserve Bank has reportedly capped its own cash lending to banks at one point

37
00:02:03,847 --> 00:02:05,167
five lakh crore rupees.

38
00:02:05,167 --> 00:02:08,429
On days when banks asked for more, it has not offered more.

39
00:02:09,073 --> 00:02:09,768
Now, why?

40
00:02:09,768 --> 00:02:10,674
The rupee.

41
00:02:10,674 --> 00:02:16,430
It has been weak, at around ninety-five to the dollar, and it came close to a record

42
00:02:16,430 --> 00:02:17,634
low earlier this month.

43
00:02:17,634 --> 00:02:19,560
Here is how tight cash can help.

44
00:02:19,560 --> 00:02:21,882
Think of someone betting that the rupee will fall.

45
00:02:21,882 --> 00:02:24,565
They sell rupees and buy dollars.

46
00:02:24,565 --> 00:02:28,859
Such a bet is often funded by borrowing rupees for short periods.

47
00:02:28,859 --> 00:02:34,728
If overnight money gets dearer, every night of that bet costs more, so it has to pay

48
00:02:34,728 --> 00:02:35,444
off faster.

49
00:02:35,444 --> 00:02:39,069
That makes betting against the rupee less attractive.

50
00:02:39,601 --> 00:02:42,759
One analyst says the tightness looks intentional, for just this reason.

51
00:02:42,759 --> 00:02:44,873
The Reserve Bank has not said so.

52
00:02:44,873 --> 00:02:50,353
Its Governor said last week that monetary policy can at times be used to address

53
00:02:50,353 --> 00:02:52,107
financial stability risks.

54
00:02:52,686 --> 00:02:55,858
There is another way to defend a currency:

55
00:02:55,858 --> 00:02:58,649
sell dollars from the country's reserves.

56
00:02:58,649 --> 00:03:01,070
But reserves are finite.

57
00:03:01,070 --> 00:03:03,883
This is an alternative that does not spend them.

58
00:03:03,883 --> 00:03:08,058
That is our inference, not something the Reserve Bank has stated.

59
00:03:08,626 --> 00:03:10,140
What does it mean for India?

60
00:03:10,140 --> 00:03:12,306
Money is getting costlier.

61
00:03:12,306 --> 00:03:17,565
Banks are paying about half a percentage point more on fresh short-term certificates

62
00:03:17,565 --> 00:03:22,644
of deposit, which are short-term borrowing notes that banks issue, and that is a

63
00:03:22,644 --> 00:03:23,543
seven-month high.

64
00:03:23,543 --> 00:03:28,089
One likely effect is that loans become costlier over time.

65
00:03:28,089 --> 00:03:33,329
On the other side, a steadier rupee helps keep import bills, including oil, in

66
00:03:33,329 --> 00:03:33,929
check.

67
00:03:34,178 --> 00:03:35,443
So, a quick recap:

68
00:03:35,443 --> 00:03:40,477
the Reserve Bank has pulled cash out of the banks, and the rupee is the likely

69
00:03:40,477 --> 00:03:41,077
reason.

70
00:03:41,507 --> 00:03:42,579
What do we not know?

71
00:03:42,579 --> 00:03:46,440
The Reserve Bank has not announced a purpose for the tight cash.

72
00:03:46,440 --> 00:03:51,438
The liquidity figure is an estimate, not the Reserve Bank's own number.

73
00:03:51,438 --> 00:03:53,565
And the rupee moves every day.

74
00:03:53,565 --> 00:03:58,593
The next policy meeting is from the second to the fourth of December.

75
00:03:59,288 --> 00:04:00,161
To sum up:

76
00:04:00,161 --> 00:04:05,401
the Reserve Bank is defending the rupee by making money scarce, not only by selling

77
00:04:05,401 --> 00:04:09,949
dollars, and the price is costlier borrowing at home.

78
00:04:09,949 --> 00:04:12,636
Thanks for listening, and see you in the next episode!

